# Everything outside the course tree that changes when the installer REFUSES an unsupported
# distribution. This is the shortest expected set in this directory, and that is the claim.
#
#   /work   the read-only mount this harness adds, not the installer
#   C /etc  podman bind-mounts hosts/hostname/resolv.conf into every container, which moves the
#           directory's mtime. Present in every case here.
#   C /tmp  the bootstrap's scratch directory, created and removed. See below -- this line is
#           the one interesting thing in this file.
#
# ─── the line that was added, and the principle it does not break  (#221) ──────
#
# THIS FILE USED TO SAY "NOTHING ELSE AT ALL", and the sentence was load-bearing rather than
# decorative: the ordering was the point, because a machine this script cannot support should be
# told before it is asked for permission and before anything is inspected on its behalf -- the
# same order say_intel_mac refuses an Intel Mac in.
#
# The split changed the order. install-cs193v.sh has to download the course files before there is
# any course code to run, so `mktemp -d /tmp/cs193v-install.XXXXXX` and an unpack now happen
# before survey has looked at anything. That is a real reversal and worth being explicit about
# rather than quietly relisting.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES IS THE PROPERTY THAT WAS ALWAYS MEANT: nothing is done TO this machine before it
# is told it is unsupported. A scratch directory under /tmp that is created, read and removed
# within one run is not a change to someone's computer -- no package, no /etc file, no course
# tree, and nothing left behind. The rest of this file is still the assertion that says so, and
# it is still the shortest set here.
#
# MEASURED, so the single line is not an assumption: creating and removing one child of /tmp in
# a container yields exactly `C /tmp` and nothing else -- the directory's mtime moved, and the
# child is not in the diff because it no longer exists. If the removal ever regressed, this file
# would grow the whole unpacked tree under a random name, which is how the leak was found; and
# course-install.sh's boot_cleanup, 25-installer.sh's install:the-bootstrap-temp-tree-is-removed
# and 26-installer-sandbox.sh's ===BOOT-TMP=== checks are the three that name it directly.
#
# STILL NOTABLY ABSENT, and this is the comparison that used to be drawn against a file that does
# not exist (expected-system-paths.debian, cited here for a while and never written): the /tmp
# runtime directories a real podman creates. expected-system-paths.podman-old carries
# /tmp/containers-user-1000 and /tmp/podman-run-1000, all from the single `podman --version` its
# survey runs. This refusal never reaches that probe -- distro_family() returns `unsupported`,
# survey says so at "Looking at your computer" and exits -- so podman is never executed. If a
# future change moved the refusal later, into install_podman say, this file would grow those
# directories and say so.
A /work
C /etc
C /tmp
